// title: Device code sign-ins in the last 30 days, which is what EvilTokens industrialised
// date: 2026-09-25
// product: Entra
// tables: SigninLogs
// purpose: Microsoft's Digital Crimes Unit disrupted EvilTokens on 22 September; the platform's whole product was device code phishing. Device code flow is still enabled by default in many tenants and produces a refresh token with no phishing-resistant factor anywhere in the path, so the population that uses i
// source: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/reference/tables/signinlogs
// archived by tools/kql_archive.py (CLAUDE.md 5l)
// Microsoft SOC Brief - 25 September 2026
// Who is actually using device code flow, after the EvilTokens disruption of 22 September 2026.
// Table and columns verified against the SigninLogs reference on 25 September 2026:
// https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-monitor/reference/tables/signinlogs
// AuthenticationProtocol, ResultType, UserPrincipalName, AppDisplayName, IPAddress,
// Location and ClientAppUsed are all columns of that table.
SigninLogs
| where TimeGenerated > ago(30d)
| where AuthenticationProtocol == "deviceCode"
| extend Outcome = iff(ResultType == "0", "success", "failure")
| summarize Signins = count(),
            Successes = countif(Outcome == "success"),
            Apps = make_set(AppDisplayName, 12),
            Countries = make_set(Location, 12),
            Addresses = dcount(IPAddress),
            FirstSeen = min(TimeGenerated),
            LastSeen  = max(TimeGenerated)
        by UserPrincipalName
| where Successes > 0
| sort by Signins desc
// Read the country and address spread first: device code flow used from one office is a tool,
// the same account succeeding from several countries in a month is the EvilTokens pattern.
